<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1125113" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2025/10/22203221/Georgia_OKeeffe_-_Lake_George_Reflection.jpg" alt width="1600" height="937" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2025/10/22203221/Georgia_OKeeffe_-_Lake_George_Reflection.jpg 1600w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2025/10/22203221/Georgia_OKeeffe_-_Lake_George_Reflection-360x211.jpg 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2025/10/22203221/Georgia_OKeeffe_-_Lake_George_Reflection-1024x600.jpg 1024w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2025/10/22203221/Georgia_OKeeffe_-_Lake_George_Reflection-240x141.jpg 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2025/10/22203221/Georgia_OKeeffe_-_Lake_George_Reflection-768x450.jpg 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2025/10/22203221/Georgia_OKeeffe_-_Lake_George_Reflection-1536x900.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1600px) 100vw, 1600px"></p>
<p>Upon hearing the names of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Dove">Arthur Dove</a> or <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsden_Hartley">Marsden Hartley,</a> the saturated colors and organically askew lines of those painters’ landscapes may appear before your mind’s eye. But unless you have a special interest in American modernists of the early twentieth century, they probably don’t. The name <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_O%27Keeffe">Georgia O’Keeffe</a>, by contrast, can hardly fail to bring a few images even to the mind of the strictly casual art appreciator: New Mexican mesas, animal skulls, and above all flowers in extreme close-up. Apart from the artistic skill and distinctive vision with which she created it, O’Keeffe’s work persists in the wider culture because of how well it happens to reproduce in a variety of contexts, including postcards, mugs, and even apparel, such as that <a href="https://www.okeeffemuseum.org/products/?_product_category=apparel-accessories">sold at her eponymous museum in Santa Fe</a>.</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1126735" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/03/22232755/Georgia_OKeeffe_Red_Canna_1919_HMA.jpg" alt width="553" height="768" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/03/22232755/Georgia_OKeeffe_Red_Canna_1919_HMA.jpg 553w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/03/22232755/Georgia_OKeeffe_Red_Canna_1919_HMA-259x360.jpg 259w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/03/22232755/Georgia_OKeeffe_Red_Canna_1919_HMA-173x240.jpg 173w" sizes="(max-width: 553px) 100vw, 553px"></p>
<p>Keeping such products around is, of course, no substitute for seeing the real thing; in their physical reality, O’Keeffe’s paintings have a way of rebuffing all the interpretations with which they’ve been freighted for more than a century now. If you can’t make it out to New Mexico, the Georgia O’Keeffe Museum has been working to make every single one of her pieces (including sculptures and photographs) available for viewing online at a just-launched portal called <strong><a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/">Access O’Keeffe</a></strong>.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p><a href="https://www.okeeffemuseum.org/news/access-okeeffe-to-launch-in-early-2026/">The museum describes it</a> as a “user-friendly, searchable <a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/">website</a> with high-resolution images, visual descriptions, exhibition histories, archival materials, and research data associated with the artist’s two-volume catalogue raisonné.” <a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/">The sit</a>e’s visitors “can browse by color, shape, or medium, explore the context of works created before and after a specific painting, trace historic exhibitions, create lists of favorites, and download images.”</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1126734" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/03/22232647/Georgia_OKeeffe_Series_1_No._8.jpg" alt width="557" height="682" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/03/22232647/Georgia_OKeeffe_Series_1_No._8.jpg 557w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/03/22232647/Georgia_OKeeffe_Series_1_No._8-294x360.jpg 294w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/03/22232647/Georgia_OKeeffe_Series_1_No._8-196x240.jpg 196w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 557px) 100vw, 557px"></p>
<p><a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/">Access O’Keeffe</a> makes it easy to find the artist’s most famous paintings, but also works that may surprise viewers who only know her mesas, skulls, and flowers. Take, for example, such nocturnally themed canvases as her early <a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/object/1092/"><em>Starlight Night</em></a>, from 1917, or her late <a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/object/1530/"><em>Untitled (City Night)</em></a>, from the nineteen-seventies. O’Keeffe’s America, we must remember, isn’t limited to the desert: though she did spend most of her nearly century-long life’s second half in New Mexico, it&nbsp;also took her from Wisconsin to Virginia to Texas to New York, with stints in South Carolina and Hawaii. Given the importance of understanding any artist’s contexts both geographical and social,&nbsp;<a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/">Access O’Keeffe</a> also provides <a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/actor/">an archive of artifacts and exhibitions related to the people and organizations associated with her</a>&nbsp;— <a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/search/?s=Arthur%20Dove">Arthur Dove</a> and <a href="https://access-ok.okeeffemuseum.org/search/?s=Marsden%20Hartley">Marsden Hartley</a> included.</p>
